WEBVTT

00:00:00.043 --> 00:00:01.044 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
GridBot Builder.

00:00:01.634 --> 00:00:02.784 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
GridBot Blueprint.

00:00:03.340 --> 00:00:04.074 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Lesson one.

00:00:04.626 --> 00:00:05.355 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Introduction.

00:00:06.470 --> 00:00:07.616 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Education only.

00:00:08.175 --> 00:00:09.489 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Not financial advice.

00:00:10.009 --> 00:00:12.061 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
This is general information and

00:00:12.061 --> 00:00:14.264 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
doesn't consider your objectives,

00:00:14.264 --> 00:00:16.164 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
financial situation or needs.

00:00:16.684 --> 00:00:18.883 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Crypto, a digital coin, is high risk;

00:00:18.883 --> 00:00:21.319 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
you could lose all the money you put in.

00:00:21.839 --> 00:00:23.904 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Past performance is not a reliable

00:00:23.904 --> 00:00:25.613 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
indicator of future results.

00:00:26.663 --> 00:00:29.056 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
The prices are public historical prices

00:00:29.056 --> 00:00:31.234 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
at the end of each day, marked Example.

00:00:32.367 --> 00:00:34.266 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
No order is sent from this lesson.

00:00:35.265 --> 00:00:37.241 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
By the end of this series you will

00:00:37.241 --> 00:00:39.291 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
understand what a grid bot, a small

00:00:39.291 --> 00:00:41.340 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
program that places these orders

00:00:41.340 --> 00:00:43.243 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
for you, does, when it can capture

00:00:43.243 --> 00:00:44.854 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
bounces, and where it loses.

00:00:45.374 --> 00:00:47.384 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
You will know how to plan one, test

00:00:47.384 --> 00:00:49.284 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
it on paper, run it, check it, and

00:00:49.284 --> 00:00:51.331 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
change the capital you give to bots

00:00:51.331 --> 00:00:53.193 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
only under written risk rules.

00:00:53.713 --> 00:00:55.110 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
This is education, not

00:00:55.110 --> 00:00:56.429 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
a promise of results.

00:00:56.949 --> 00:00:58.560 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Price often moves sideways,

00:00:58.560 --> 00:01:00.310 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
bouncing between the same top

00:01:00.310 --> 00:01:02.061 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
and bottom instead of leaving.

00:01:02.581 --> 00:01:04.290 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
A grid is a ladder of waiting

00:01:04.290 --> 00:01:06.000 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
orders, called limit orders,

00:01:06.000 --> 00:01:07.487 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
built for that bouncing.

00:01:08.007 --> 00:01:09.850 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
When price runs one way and stays

00:01:09.850 --> 00:01:12.029 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
there, the grid has nothing left to do.

00:01:12.410 --> 00:01:14.163 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
And if price falls through the

00:01:14.163 --> 00:01:15.846 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
bottom, coin means the crypto

00:01:15.846 --> 00:01:17.669 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
itself, and you are left holding

00:01:17.669 --> 00:01:19.401 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
the coin, and it is worth less.

00:01:20.331 --> 00:01:21.672 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
By the end you will know

00:01:21.672 --> 00:01:22.957 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
what a grid bot places.

00:01:24.086 --> 00:01:26.165 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
You will see that idea on

00:01:26.165 --> 00:01:28.140 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
one real path from 2023.

00:01:29.340 --> 00:01:30.848 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
You will be able to say the

00:01:30.848 --> 00:01:32.301 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
stop rule in one sentence.

00:01:33.255 --> 00:01:35.101 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
When a chart says Example, it

00:01:35.101 --> 00:01:36.946 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
means a real past price used

00:01:36.946 --> 00:01:38.792 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
as an example, not a forecast.

00:01:39.842 --> 00:01:41.084 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Crypto is a digital

00:01:41.084 --> 00:01:42.481 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
asset, such as bitcoin.

00:01:43.001 --> 00:01:44.677 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
You do not need to have bought

00:01:44.677 --> 00:01:45.962 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
any before this lesson.

00:01:46.341 --> 00:01:48.308 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
An exchange is the website or app

00:01:48.308 --> 00:01:50.640 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
where you swap Australian dollars for

00:01:50.640 --> 00:01:52.681 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
crypto, and crypto back to dollars.

00:01:53.201 --> 00:01:54.877 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
This course does not name one,

00:01:54.877 --> 00:01:56.553 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
and it does not send an order.

00:01:57.298 --> 00:01:58.862 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Cash is money you can spend.

00:01:59.264 --> 00:02:00.886 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Here that means dollars, or a

00:02:00.886 --> 00:02:02.650 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
dollar-pegged token, treated

00:02:02.650 --> 00:02:04.060 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
like cash in this lesson.

00:02:04.580 --> 00:02:05.596 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
The peg can break.

00:02:06.116 --> 00:02:08.308 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Coin is the crypto, such as bitcoin.

00:02:09.358 --> 00:02:11.704 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
An order is an instruction to buy or sell.

00:02:12.156 --> 00:02:13.964 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
A buy order swaps cash for coin.

00:02:14.484 --> 00:02:16.386 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
A sell order swaps coin back to

00:02:16.386 --> 00:02:18.518 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
cash, and you can only sell coin you

00:02:18.518 --> 00:02:20.420 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
already hold, on a spot account.

00:02:20.940 --> 00:02:22.577 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
A spot account is the normal

00:02:22.577 --> 00:02:23.929 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
account on an exchange.

00:02:24.449 --> 00:02:25.846 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
You own the coin you buy,

00:02:25.846 --> 00:02:27.187 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
and nothing is borrowed.

00:02:28.069 --> 00:02:29.838 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
A bot is software that places

00:02:29.838 --> 00:02:31.460 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
and refills orders for you.

00:02:31.980 --> 00:02:33.824 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
You start with some cash and some

00:02:33.824 --> 00:02:35.500 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
coin, so there is something to

00:02:35.500 --> 00:02:37.232 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
buy with and something to sell.

00:02:37.281 --> 00:02:39.645 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
A grid bot places waiting buy orders

00:02:39.645 --> 00:02:41.774 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
on lines below the price and sell

00:02:41.774 --> 00:02:43.902 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
orders on lines above it, inside a

00:02:43.902 --> 00:02:45.636 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
top and a bottom you choose.

00:02:46.156 --> 00:02:47.832 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
The space between your top and

00:02:47.832 --> 00:02:49.620 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
your bottom is called the range.

00:02:49.714 --> 00:02:51.819 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
The sells need some coin, so a grid

00:02:51.819 --> 00:02:53.768 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
starts with part cash and part

00:02:53.768 --> 00:02:55.718 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
coin, and that is a description,

00:02:55.718 --> 00:02:57.589 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
not advice about what to hold.

00:02:58.639 --> 00:03:00.345 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
A limit order is an order that

00:03:00.345 --> 00:03:01.910 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
waits at a price you choose.

00:03:02.429 --> 00:03:03.993 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
It does not chase the price.

00:03:04.413 --> 00:03:05.977 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Chasing the price would mean

00:03:05.977 --> 00:03:07.430 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
moving the order to follow

00:03:07.430 --> 00:03:08.994 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
wherever the price has gone.

00:03:09.843 --> 00:03:11.918 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
An order fills when the price reaches

00:03:11.918 --> 00:03:13.650 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
its line and the trade happens.

00:03:14.111 --> 00:03:16.318 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Until then, the order is only waiting.

00:03:17.368 --> 00:03:19.323 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Think of a ladder leaned on a wall.

00:03:19.465 --> 00:03:21.811 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
The rungs wait, and they do not chase you.

00:03:22.087 --> 00:03:24.377 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
A rung is one price where an order waits.

00:03:24.784 --> 00:03:26.963 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
The gap is the space between two rungs.

00:03:27.252 --> 00:03:29.297 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
The ladder is the analogy, and the

00:03:29.297 --> 00:03:31.568 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
chart beside it is the real past path.

00:03:32.618 --> 00:03:34.029 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
The range is the top rung

00:03:34.029 --> 00:03:35.158 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
and the bottom rung.

00:03:35.678 --> 00:03:37.801 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
The levels are the rungs between them.

00:03:38.151 --> 00:03:40.441 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
The stop is the written rule for leaving.

00:03:41.290 --> 00:03:43.134 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
The range is the top line and the

00:03:43.134 --> 00:03:44.977 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
bottom line, taken from the path.

00:03:45.456 --> 00:03:46.944 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Hindsight: this range is

00:03:46.944 --> 00:03:48.209 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
drawn after the fact.

00:03:48.729 --> 00:03:51.953 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
The chart is bitcoin's real daily

00:03:51.953 --> 00:03:55.523 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
closes from 19 July to 16 October 2023.

00:03:56.043 --> 00:03:57.495 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Every close in this window

00:03:57.495 --> 00:03:59.227 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
stayed between those two lines.

00:04:00.013 --> 00:04:02.419 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Six gaps of about 900 dollars each sit

00:04:02.419 --> 00:04:04.980 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
between the bottom line and the top line.

00:04:05.500 --> 00:04:07.727 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Each gap is about 900 dollars:

00:04:07.727 --> 00:04:09.954 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
the top line minus the bottom

00:04:09.954 --> 00:04:11.439 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
line, divided by six.

00:04:11.959 --> 00:04:13.304 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
It is one step between

00:04:13.304 --> 00:04:14.873 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
lines, not the whole range.

00:04:15.393 --> 00:04:17.231 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Seven lines make those six gaps.

00:04:17.751 --> 00:04:19.539 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
The levels are the prices on the

00:04:19.539 --> 00:04:21.215 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
rungs where limit orders wait.

00:04:22.202 --> 00:04:24.492 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Buy orders wait on lines below the price.

00:04:24.928 --> 00:04:26.883 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Sell orders wait on lines above it.

00:04:27.365 --> 00:04:29.864 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Each fill changes what you hold: a buy

00:04:29.864 --> 00:04:32.530 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
fill adds coin, and a sell fill adds cash.

00:04:33.050 --> 00:04:34.223 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
You choose the range,

00:04:34.223 --> 00:04:35.508 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
the gaps and the money.

00:04:35.817 --> 00:04:37.269 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
The bot places each order.

00:04:38.276 --> 00:04:40.069 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
After a fill, the bot places the

00:04:40.069 --> 00:04:41.790 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
opposite order one rung away.

00:04:42.310 --> 00:04:44.488 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
A buy fill gets a sell one rung higher.

00:04:44.875 --> 00:04:47.105 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
A sell fill gets a buy one rung lower.

00:04:47.625 --> 00:04:49.245 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
You still write the stop, and

00:04:49.245 --> 00:04:50.921 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
you act when that rule is met.

00:04:51.152 --> 00:04:53.387 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
The bot does not decide the stop for you

00:04:53.387 --> 00:04:55.510 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
unless you turned that setting on, and

00:04:55.510 --> 00:04:57.577 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
you write which one before you start.

00:04:58.311 --> 00:04:59.652 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Think of a market stall.

00:05:00.084 --> 00:05:02.198 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
The stallholder buys stock a little

00:05:02.198 --> 00:05:04.312 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
cheaper and sells it a little dearer,

00:05:04.312 --> 00:05:05.932 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
and pays a fee on every sale.

00:05:06.383 --> 00:05:07.952 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Exchanges charge a small

00:05:07.952 --> 00:05:08.998 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
fee on each trade.

00:05:09.518 --> 00:05:11.261 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
A grid can do that while price

00:05:11.261 --> 00:05:12.787 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
bounces inside the range.

00:05:13.307 --> 00:05:15.828 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
A bounce means price moves down and back

00:05:15.828 --> 00:05:18.197 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
up, or up and back down, and stays inside.

00:05:18.717 --> 00:05:20.953 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
One buy at a lower line and the later

00:05:20.953 --> 00:05:23.036 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
sell of that same coin is one pair.

00:05:23.556 --> 00:05:25.708 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
On these daily closes, the July to

00:05:25.708 --> 00:05:27.940 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
October example window finished

00:05:27.940 --> 00:05:30.012 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
only three pairs one rung apart.

00:05:30.532 --> 00:05:32.264 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
The sell line is higher, so the

00:05:32.264 --> 00:05:34.108 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
cash that comes back is more than

00:05:34.108 --> 00:05:36.063 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
the cash that left, before the fee.

00:05:36.356 --> 00:05:37.809 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
A fee is what the exchange

00:05:37.809 --> 00:05:39.373 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
charges when an order fills.

00:05:39.554 --> 00:05:41.174 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
The fee comes out of the gap.

00:05:41.401 --> 00:05:43.133 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
If the fees are larger than the

00:05:43.133 --> 00:05:44.697 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
gap, the pair keeps nothing.

00:05:44.878 --> 00:05:46.121 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
This is not a promise.

00:05:46.641 --> 00:05:48.445 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
The pair finishes only if price

00:05:48.445 --> 00:05:50.318 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
does bounce and both orders fill.

00:05:51.368 --> 00:05:52.597 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
The daily close is the

00:05:52.597 --> 00:05:53.826 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
last price of the day.

00:05:54.247 --> 00:05:56.047 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Crypto trades all day, so the

00:05:56.047 --> 00:05:57.456 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
day is cut at a set time.

00:05:57.976 --> 00:06:00.835 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
The exchange's day ends at 11am Sydney

00:06:00.835 --> 00:06:03.783 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
time in daylight saving, 10am otherwise.

00:06:04.303 --> 00:06:06.287 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
This is a simple candle that shows

00:06:06.287 --> 00:06:07.963 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
the day's high, low and close.

00:06:08.437 --> 00:06:10.366 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Traders call it a candle because

00:06:10.366 --> 00:06:12.295 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
the full version has a thick body

00:06:12.295 --> 00:06:14.027 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
and a thin wick, like a candle.

00:06:14.459 --> 00:06:15.967 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Here we only need the high,

00:06:15.967 --> 00:06:17.196 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
the low and the close.

00:06:17.530 --> 00:06:20.709 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
This candle is 19 July 2023.

00:06:21.229 --> 00:06:22.459 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
The high, the low, and

00:06:22.459 --> 00:06:23.743 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
the close are labelled.

00:06:24.636 --> 00:06:26.766 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
The example is Bitcoin's public

00:06:26.766 --> 00:06:28.421 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
daily price in US dollars.

00:06:28.941 --> 00:06:32.119 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
From 19 July 2023 to 16 October

00:06:32.119 --> 00:06:35.042 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
2023, every one of those days

00:06:35.042 --> 00:06:37.585 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
closed inside the range.

00:06:38.105 --> 00:06:39.778 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
The close crossed the seven

00:06:39.778 --> 00:06:41.524 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
lines 28 times in that window.

00:06:42.044 --> 00:06:44.355 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
The count is an example, not a forecast.

00:06:44.875 --> 00:06:46.663 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
We do not need to read each day.

00:06:46.929 --> 00:06:48.272 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
The shape is the lesson,

00:06:48.272 --> 00:06:49.685 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
and it is marked Example.

00:06:50.205 --> 00:06:52.961 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Worked check: 19 July

00:06:52.961 --> 00:06:56.042 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
2023 to 16 October 2023.

00:06:56.562 --> 00:06:58.070 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
The low is the bottom line.

00:06:58.550 --> 00:07:00.016 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
The high is the top line.

00:07:00.536 --> 00:07:02.380 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Nothing on this card is an order.

00:07:03.343 --> 00:07:06.186 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Those 28 crossings, each time the price

00:07:06.186 --> 00:07:08.585 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
crosses a line, are not 28 results.

00:07:09.105 --> 00:07:11.307 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
A crossing only means the close moved

00:07:11.307 --> 00:07:13.374 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
from one side of a line to the other.

00:07:13.816 --> 00:07:15.528 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
A pair is finished only when a

00:07:15.528 --> 00:07:17.382 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
buy and its later sell have both

00:07:17.382 --> 00:07:19.113 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
filled, and only after the fee.

00:07:19.613 --> 00:07:21.512 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
The price can cross many lines and

00:07:21.512 --> 00:07:23.412 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
still leave you holding coin if it

00:07:23.412 --> 00:07:25.032 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
walks out through the bottom.

00:07:25.568 --> 00:07:27.077 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
You can buy part of a coin.

00:07:27.626 --> 00:07:30.585 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Here we buy one hundredth

00:07:30.626 --> 00:07:31.687 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
of a bitcoin, 0.01.

00:07:31.687 --> 00:07:34.950 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
900 dollars times 0.01 bitcoin

00:07:34.950 --> 00:07:37.474 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
is 9 dollars before fees.

00:07:37.994 --> 00:07:40.975 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
In this example a buy waiting at the

00:07:40.975 --> 00:07:44.264 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
lower of these two lines was reached on

00:07:44.264 --> 00:07:47.245 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
17 August 2023, and the sell one line

00:07:47.245 --> 00:07:50.226 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
above was reached on 29 August 2023.

00:07:50.746 --> 00:07:52.534 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
The card shows the gap, then the

00:07:52.534 --> 00:07:54.098 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
two fees, then what is left.

00:07:54.407 --> 00:07:56.231 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
That is about 8 dollars 46

00:07:56.231 --> 00:07:58.317 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
after fees, for one small pair.

00:07:58.837 --> 00:08:00.736 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
It happens only if the price comes

00:08:00.736 --> 00:08:02.412 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
back up through the sell line.

00:08:02.516 --> 00:08:04.248 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
If the price leaves through the

00:08:04.248 --> 00:08:06.036 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
bottom, you still hold the coin,

00:08:06.036 --> 00:08:07.600 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
and the pair never finishes.

00:08:07.792 --> 00:08:09.580 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
This card is not money you made.

00:08:09.978 --> 00:08:12.324 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Past results don't predict future results.

00:08:19.312 --> 00:08:21.100 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Now watch what you hold when the

00:08:21.100 --> 00:08:23.167 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
price leaves the range and stays out.

00:08:23.547 --> 00:08:25.223 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Above the range the sells have

00:08:25.223 --> 00:08:27.010 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
filled and you are holding cash,

00:08:27.010 --> 00:08:28.463 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
with nothing left to sell.

00:08:28.785 --> 00:08:30.405 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Below the range the buys have

00:08:30.405 --> 00:08:31.857 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
filled and you are holding

00:08:31.857 --> 00:08:33.589 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
coin, with nothing left to buy.

00:08:33.967 --> 00:08:35.359 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
That is why the way down

00:08:35.359 --> 00:08:37.117 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
matters most: every buy fills,

00:08:37.117 --> 00:08:38.875 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
and you are left holding coin.

00:08:40.925 --> 00:08:43.160 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Here is that stop rule on the real path.

00:08:43.581 --> 00:08:45.926 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
It triggers if a daily close is outside

00:08:45.926 --> 00:08:48.271 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
the range, through the top or the bottom,

00:08:48.271 --> 00:08:50.762 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
and the next daily close is still outside.

00:08:51.282 --> 00:08:52.735 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Then the idea is finished.

00:08:53.115 --> 00:08:55.208 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
You stop the bot, which cancels the

00:08:55.208 --> 00:08:57.078 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
waiting orders, and do what you

00:08:57.078 --> 00:08:59.172 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
wrote: keep the coin or swap to cash.

00:08:59.692 --> 00:09:01.673 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
A bot's automatic stop is a price

00:09:01.673 --> 00:09:03.361 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
that can fire during the day.

00:09:03.881 --> 00:09:05.581 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
This course uses the two daily

00:09:05.581 --> 00:09:07.416 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
closes, unless your written plan

00:09:07.416 --> 00:09:09.204 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
says you turned on a price stop.

00:09:09.636 --> 00:09:10.977 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
This is a teaching rule,

00:09:10.977 --> 00:09:12.155 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
not a recommendation.

00:09:12.675 --> 00:09:14.295 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
The stop is your written rule

00:09:14.295 --> 00:09:15.747 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
for when you stop the bot.

00:09:15.980 --> 00:09:18.114 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
On 23 October 2023 the close

00:09:18.114 --> 00:09:20.526 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
finished above the top line, and

00:09:20.526 --> 00:09:22.845 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
the next day was still above it.

00:09:27.895 --> 00:09:29.347 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Through the top, the sells

00:09:29.347 --> 00:09:31.023 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
have filled and you hold cash.

00:09:31.132 --> 00:09:32.976 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Through the bottom, the buys have

00:09:32.976 --> 00:09:34.819 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
filled and you hold coin that you

00:09:34.819 --> 00:09:36.831 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
bought above where the price is now.

00:09:37.190 --> 00:09:38.642 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
That loss can keep growing

00:09:38.642 --> 00:09:39.759 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
until the stop acts.

00:09:40.064 --> 00:09:41.908 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
You stop the bot either way, then

00:09:41.908 --> 00:09:43.472 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
you follow the written rule.

00:09:43.581 --> 00:09:45.369 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
The bot does not decide the stop

00:09:45.369 --> 00:09:47.268 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
unless you turned that setting on.

00:09:47.541 --> 00:09:48.963 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
EXAMPLE, made up: three

00:09:48.963 --> 00:09:50.542 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
buys fill on the way down.

00:09:51.062 --> 00:09:54.362 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
One hundredth of a bitcoin

00:09:54.362 --> 00:09:57.661 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
at 27,630 is 276 dollars 30.

00:09:58.515 --> 00:10:04.049 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
One hundredth at 26,730 is 267 dollars 30.

00:10:04.901 --> 00:10:10.083 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
One hundredth at 25,830 is 258 dollars 30.

00:10:10.603 --> 00:10:13.243 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Together that is 801 dollars 90.

00:10:14.006 --> 00:10:18.213 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Three buys of 0.01 is 0.03 bitcoin.

00:10:18.837 --> 00:10:22.023 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
At 24,000, that is 720 dollars.

00:10:22.543 --> 00:10:24.809 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
A little below the range, at a

00:10:24.809 --> 00:10:27.175 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
made-up price of 24,000, that

00:10:27.175 --> 00:10:29.245 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
coin is worth 720 dollars.

00:10:29.765 --> 00:10:31.391 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
You are down 81 dollars 90

00:10:31.391 --> 00:10:33.326 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
before fees, and the loss keeps

00:10:33.326 --> 00:10:35.185 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
growing until your stop acts.

00:10:36.235 --> 00:10:37.810 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
A common mistake is thinking

00:10:37.810 --> 00:10:39.318 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
the bot predicts the price.

00:10:40.107 --> 00:10:40.857 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
It does not.

00:10:41.423 --> 00:10:42.205 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
It only waits.

00:10:42.575 --> 00:10:44.083 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Another mistake is starting

00:10:44.083 --> 00:10:45.257 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
with no written stop.

00:10:45.671 --> 00:10:46.900 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
There is no promise of

00:10:46.900 --> 00:10:48.185 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
results in this lesson.

00:10:48.376 --> 00:10:49.984 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Numbers marked Example are

00:10:49.984 --> 00:10:51.662 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
teaching numbers, not advice.

00:10:52.712 --> 00:10:54.976 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Paper or demo trading is like driving

00:10:54.976 --> 00:10:56.947 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
with learner plates, or training

00:10:56.947 --> 00:10:58.992 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
wheels: you practise with pretend

00:10:58.992 --> 00:11:00.817 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
money before anything is real.

00:11:01.337 --> 00:11:02.854 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Lesson 7 shows you how.

00:11:03.911 --> 00:11:05.038 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Steps one to six.

00:11:05.738 --> 00:11:07.426 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
This introduction is step one.

00:11:08.126 --> 00:11:09.188 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Then what you need.

00:11:09.818 --> 00:11:11.345 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Then exactly what a grid

00:11:11.345 --> 00:11:12.796 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
bot does, order by order.

00:11:13.796 --> 00:11:14.913 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Then market regimes.

00:11:15.489 --> 00:11:17.556 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Then choosing grid spacing and range.

00:11:18.195 --> 00:11:19.802 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Then entry and exit rules.

00:11:20.517 --> 00:11:21.817 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Steps seven to eleven.

00:11:22.517 --> 00:11:23.601 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Then a paper test.

00:11:24.322 --> 00:11:25.384 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Then weekly checks.

00:11:26.121 --> 00:11:27.309 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Then common mistakes.

00:11:28.009 --> 00:11:30.020 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Then scaling up under written rules.

00:11:30.481 --> 00:11:31.871 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Then an opt-in lesson on

00:11:31.871 --> 00:11:33.957 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
liquidation and your complete plan.

00:11:35.007 --> 00:11:36.907 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
The next lesson is a checklist you

00:11:36.907 --> 00:11:38.806 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
write on paper, before any screen.

00:11:39.828 --> 00:11:42.205 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
In short: a grid waits, it buys lower and

00:11:42.205 --> 00:11:44.582 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
sells higher while price bounces, the

00:11:44.582 --> 00:11:46.882 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
fees come out of the gap, and if a daily

00:11:46.882 --> 00:11:49.335 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
close leaves and stays out, you stop the

00:11:49.335 --> 00:11:51.406 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
bot and follow the rule you wrote.

00:11:52.490 --> 00:11:53.356 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Check yourself.

00:11:53.876 --> 00:11:55.328 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Why does a grid start with

00:11:55.328 --> 00:11:56.781 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
some coin as well as cash?

00:11:56.944 --> 00:11:58.229 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Because the sell orders

00:11:58.229 --> 00:11:59.738 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
need coin you already hold.

00:12:00.755 --> 00:12:03.477 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
In the example pair, why is the net about

00:12:03.477 --> 00:12:05.773 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
8 dollars 46 and not the whole gap?

00:12:06.293 --> 00:12:07.857 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
The two fees come out first.

00:12:09.020 --> 00:12:11.087 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Price closes above your top line, and

00:12:11.087 --> 00:12:13.265 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
the next day's close is still above it.

00:12:13.573 --> 00:12:15.361 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
What do you do, and who does it?

00:12:15.681 --> 00:12:17.469 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
You stop the bot and follow your

00:12:17.469 --> 00:12:19.313 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
written rule, by hand, unless you

00:12:19.313 --> 00:12:21.156 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
turned on the bot's stop setting.

00:12:22.213 --> 00:12:22.963 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Your turn.

00:12:23.409 --> 00:12:25.141 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
On paper, write the top and the

00:12:25.141 --> 00:12:27.096 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
bottom, one gap, and the stop rule.

00:12:28.200 --> 00:12:29.932 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Say what you will do if a daily

00:12:29.932 --> 00:12:31.608 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
close leaves the range and the

00:12:31.608 --> 00:12:33.172 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
next close is still outside.

00:12:33.546 --> 00:12:34.943 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Pause the video and write

00:12:34.943 --> 00:12:36.563 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
your answer before you go on.

00:12:37.596 --> 00:12:39.663 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
No real order is sent in this lesson.

00:12:40.150 --> 00:12:42.053 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Paper practice comes in Lesson

00:12:42.053 --> 00:12:43.663 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
7, and real money only after

00:12:43.663 --> 00:12:45.493 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
your written graduation rule.

00:12:46.013 --> 00:12:48.020 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
A graduation rule is the written

00:12:48.020 --> 00:12:49.729 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
test you pass on paper first.

00:12:50.262 --> 00:12:51.271 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
You draw the idea.

00:12:51.791 --> 00:12:53.585 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
If a screen appears later, it

00:12:53.585 --> 00:12:55.300 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
should be a copy of the page,

00:12:55.300 --> 00:12:56.860 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
and only on paper or demo.

00:12:58.060 --> 00:13:00.421 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Bring a notebook, and no secrets: no

00:13:00.421 --> 00:13:03.035 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
seed phrase, private key or API secret.

00:13:03.555 --> 00:13:05.664 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
A seed phrase or private key is the

00:13:05.664 --> 00:13:07.848 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
master password to a crypto wallet.

00:13:08.368 --> 00:13:10.433 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
An API secret is a key that lets an

00:13:10.433 --> 00:13:12.498 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
app place trades on your account.

00:13:13.548 --> 00:13:15.240 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
The pair, in the next lesson,

00:13:15.240 --> 00:13:16.855 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
is the two things you swap,

00:13:16.855 --> 00:13:18.624 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
like bitcoin and US dollars.

00:13:19.674 --> 00:13:21.903 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
A grid bot places waiting buys below

00:13:21.903 --> 00:13:23.982 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
the price and waiting sells above

00:13:23.982 --> 00:13:25.691 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
it, inside a range you choose.

00:13:26.891 --> 00:13:28.008 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
A finished pair buys

00:13:28.008 --> 00:13:29.293 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
lower and sells higher.

00:13:29.499 --> 00:13:31.119 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
The fees come out of the gap.

00:13:31.409 --> 00:13:33.467 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
In the example that was about 8

00:13:33.467 --> 00:13:35.689 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
dollars 46, and it is not a promise.

00:13:36.889 --> 00:13:38.286 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Leave through the top and

00:13:38.286 --> 00:13:39.459 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
you hold mostly cash.

00:13:39.908 --> 00:13:41.249 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Leave through the bottom

00:13:41.249 --> 00:13:42.646 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
and you hold mostly coin.

00:13:43.687 --> 00:13:45.996 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
If a daily close leaves the range and the

00:13:45.996 --> 00:13:48.305 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
next close is still outside, you stop the

00:13:48.305 --> 00:13:50.205 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
bot and follow the rule you wrote.

00:13:51.395 --> 00:13:53.629 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Next lesson: What you are going to need.

00:13:54.018 --> 00:13:55.638 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Bring your notes: your range,

00:13:55.638 --> 00:13:57.258 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
your gaps and your stop rule.

00:13:57.500 --> 00:13:58.858 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Keep the word Example on

00:13:58.858 --> 00:14:00.148 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
every practice figure.

00:14:00.668 --> 00:14:02.623 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
Next lesson you will write the page

00:14:02.623 --> 00:14:04.634 line:78% position:50% align:center size:90%
of what you need, before any screen.

